      {
        "page": 6,
        "printed": "The Example 5.3 hint states $u=\\ln u$.",
        "correction": "Use the substitution $u=\\ln x$.",
        "justification": "Differentiating $u=\\ln x$ gives $du=dx/x$, which transforms the displayed integrand $dx/[x(\\ln x)^p]$ to $u^{-p}du$.",
        "provenance": "Compared the printed hint, displayed integral, and substitution on physical page 6 of the SHA-256-matched canonical 24-page PDF."
      },
      {
        "page": 17,
        "printed": "The absolute inverse-square-root series is said to diverge “since $p<1/2$.”",
        "correction": "The exponent is $p=1/2$, and the p-series diverges because $p=1/2\\le1$.",
        "justification": "Taking absolute values gives $\\sum n^{-1/2}$; the p-series threshold is convergence exactly for $p>1$.",
        "provenance": "Compared the printed exponent inequality and displayed series on physical page 17 of the SHA-256-matched canonical 24-page PDF; recomputed the p-series criterion."
      }
    ]
  },
